# Save Our Youth — project rules

Read and follow these rules before making any change to this project.

# GLOBAL UI & MICROCOPY RULES

## 1. No Technical Provider Names in UI
Never print technical tools, infrastructure, or backend services in user-facing text unless explicitly instructed to build a developer tech-stack page.
- BAD: "Powered by Supabase", "Handled via Firebase Auth", "Secured with Stripe"
- GOOD: "Sign in", "Secure Payment", "Encrypted Connection"

## 2. Minimalist Form Microcopy
Do not explain standard UX patterns to users. If a button or form field is standard, let the UI speak for itself.
- BAD: "Click here to send us a message about your issue so we can review it."
- GOOD: "Send message"
- BAD: "Not required. Helps me with this individual project."
- GOOD: "(optional)"

## 3. No Meta-Commentary or Thought Dumping
Never output internal reasoning, prompt restatements, or setup descriptions as visible text strings in components.
- BAD: "Login page header - uses email and password"
- BAD: "Placeholder for campaign choices"
- GOOD: Leave text clean, using standard UI copy only.

## 4. Professional Copy Defaults
When writing default UI strings, write from the perspective of a polished consumer application:
- Navigation: Use clean single words ("Home", "About", "Events", "Contact").
- Buttons: Use concise action verbs ("Submit", "Join", "Save", "Continue").
- Status Messages: Keep them brief and actionable ("Account created", "Check your email").
